Root cause:
The original documentation for the `cleanup` and `closeStream` methods incorrectly specified that only `IOException` would be ignored during cleanup operations. This limited the error handling to a specific type of exception, potentially leading to unhandled `Throwable` instances, which could cause unexpected behavior or crashes in the application when other types of exceptions occurred.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
/**
 * Close the Closeable objects and <b>ignore</b> any {@link IOException} or 
```
```java
/**
 * Closes the stream ignoring {@link IOException}.
```
Reason:
The documentation incorrectly specified that only `IOException` would be ignored during cleanup operations. This limited the error handling to just `IOException`, which could lead to unhandled `Throwable` instances, potentially causing unexpected behavior or crashes when other types of exceptions occurred.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
/**
 * Close the Closeable objects and <b>ignore</b> any {@link Throwable} or
```
```java
/**
 * Closes the stream ignoring {@link Throwable}.
```
Reason:
The patch updates the documentation to specify that any `Throwable` will be ignored during cleanup operations, thereby broadening the error handling to include all types of exceptions. This change ensures that unexpected exceptions do not lead to unhandled scenarios, improving the robustness of the application.


